      <h1>Article: Ai First Work Experience</h1>
      <p class="article-subtitle">English version of: Article: Ai First Work Experience</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-content">
      <p>"No experience, how can you prove you can do it?"</p>
      <p>This is a challenge many fresh graduates face when job hunting... [Content continues in Chinese]</p>
      <p>In the past, newcomers often started with small tasks to get familiar with work：organizing materials, recording discussions, checking spreadsheets, writing first drafts。Starting slow and making mistakes，but someone could point out what was wrong。After a few times, people gradually understand one thing：what information is important, what results can be delivered，who to confirm with when encountering problems。</p>
      <p>Now, AI can organize materials, transcribe recordings, and draft content。It takes over some tasks，but this changes where newcomers start learning。If teams delegate all simple tasks to tools，without arranging new practice and mentoring，newcomers may face a practical problem：they haven't had time to learn basic work，yet are asked to deliver mature results directly。</p>
      <p>This concern is also visible in domestic graduate surveys。51job launched a special graduate survey in May 2026，receiving 6,352 company questionnaires and 18,792 individual questionnaires。Among surveyed graduates, 35.9% feel AI has replaced some traditional positions，30.2% feel AI has brought new positions，26.7% feel the number of positions hasn't changed much, but skill requirements have。Companies' responses are quite different：68.3% believe overall hiring demand is roughly stable，mainly with some position adjustments；7.3% believe basic positions are being replaced and graduate demand is decreasing。</p>
      <p>These numbers are not saying how much graduate positions have decreased nationally。Students are talking about what they feel，companies are talking about how overall hiring demand changes，They're not answering the same question。But this gap is worth noting：Companies may feel the total number hasn't changed significantly，while job seekers already feel "it's harder to get in, requirements are higher"。</p>
      <p>Job advertisements also show work opportunities are changing。PwC's analysis of mainland China job postings shows，in 2025, AI application positions increased by nearly 90,000，a year-on-year growth of 50.8%；AI development positions increased by about 30,500，a year-on-year growth of 15.5%。This refers to AI-related positions，it doesn't mean newcomer positions in all industries are increasing，nor can it prove AI application positions are graduate positions。At least, it shows the market needs more than just people who develop models，but also positions that apply AI to actual work。</p>
      <h2>Don't guess "what companies want", first see what they're actually hiring for</h2>
      <p>Many people, when anxious, start chasing the latest tools and memorizing prompts，thinking that just writing "proficient in AI" on their resume will give them more opportunities。But what recruiters usually want to know is：whether you can complete specific tasks in this job？</p>
      <p>Rather than preparing by feeling, choose a position you want to apply for，go to job sites and look at real positions。for example, select a city and job title，write down the 20 recent job postings you've seen。This number is just a small sample for your observation，it doesn't represent the entire industry。Focus on three things：</p>
      <p>After reading these job postings, you'll get a more specific question than "Will AI replace me?"：For the type of work I want to do, what's the most common and most worth practicing task?？</p>
      <h2>Create a small sample, turn "I can" into a visible process</h2>
      <p>Suppose you want to do new media operations。If job postings repeatedly mention topic selection, content organization, data observation，pick one and do a small exercise yourself：Choose a public account, record what recent content is about and who it targets，then write a suggestion on how you would improve it。</p>
      <p>AI can help you summarize content and polish your expression。But you need to keep your own judgment：Why did you choose these samples?？What information supports your conclusion?？Did AI get the content wrong?？What did you delete or modify in the end?？If the suggestion is to be implemented, what information is needed next?？</p>
      <p>Organizing it into one page is enough：What was the task, where did the materials come from, how did you do it, which step did AI help with?、What did you check and modify, what did you deliver in the end?。In interviews, don't just say "I've used AI"，you can tell a complete process：What problem did I receive, how did I find materials, what did I discover, why did I handle it this way?。</p>
      <p>For administrative, sales, design, or other work, you can use the same method，But the practice content should be selected from the job postings you're looking at。The operations example in this article is just to demonstrate the method，it doesn't mean all operations positions need to do the same thing。</p>
      <p>This kind of practice cannot replace formal work experience，nor can it guarantee getting an interview。Its use is more humble：It lets you deliver more than just a resume saying "strong learning ability"，but presents a small thing you've actually done，showing the interviewer how you think, verify, and finish things。</p>
      <p>There's also a boundary to maintain：Practice with public materials, clearly mark sources；Don't upload personal information, company materials, or unpublished documents to AI；and don't present practice as real client projects。If a recruiter asks you to complete a large real task that can be directly used for free，that's already more than just a capability test，first think clearly whether the investment is worth it。</p>
      <h2>What you can really accumulate is the ability to finish things</h2>
      <p>AI can make the first draft faster, but it can't take responsibility for results for you。Whether materials are reliable, whether numbers are read correctly、whether suggestions answer the wrong question, whose confirmation is still needed before delivery，these still need human judgment。Newcomers used to learn these by doing small tasks，now they can also actively find opportunities to practice，just that the practice needs to be more conscious, and the process needs to be documented。</p>
      <p>This doesn't mean every young person must become an AI expert。For many positions，first doing one specific job well，is more convincing than piling a row of tool names on your resume。Knowing how to ask questions is the start；being able to check answers, discover errors, and explain your choices，is what lets others see you're ready to take on work。</p>
      <p>If you're preparing to send out resumes，today pick a target position，open 20 related job postings，find a task that appears repeatedly。use public materials to make a small sample，document how you did it, how you checked it, and why you made those changes。</p>
      <p>It won't magically become your years of work experience。But when the interviewer asks "You have no experience, how can you prove you can do it?"，you can at least present something you've actually done，and explain clearly how you completed it。</p>
      <p>When you were looking for your first job, what was the hardest hurdle?？If you're already in the industry, what small thing back then made you first think "I can do this job"?？Welcome to write your experience in the comments。Your experience might just help someone who's still looking for their first step。</p>
      <p>If you know someone who's also looking for their first job，you can share this article with them，and discuss which small thing they plan to start with。</p>
      <p>数据来源：〔1〕前程无忧《2027届校招AI人才市场洞察》，2026年8月发布，本文经经济观察报报道核对... [Content continues in Chinese]</p>
    </div>

